{
  // Cloudflare Worker fronting the site root. See index.js for what it does and
  // docs/runbooks/publishing.md for how it is deployed.
  //
  // The route is claustodian.dev/* — every request on the zone reaches the
  // Worker. index.js hands back anything that is not `/` or `/mcp` untouched,
  // so the dataset is served exactly as Pages served it, with one extra hop.
  //
  // This was previously the exact root path, on the reasoning that a narrow
  // route keeps ~800 data files out of the Worker's blast radius. Two things
  // overturned that:
  //
  //  1. A Cloudflare route is matched against the whole URL including the query
  //     string, and the API rejects a `?` inside a pattern (error 10022), so an
  //     exact `/` route cannot cover `/?anything`. Measured: 20/20 bare
  //     negotiated, 0/20 with a query string.
  //  2. More decisively, a third-party readiness scanner's Markdown probe never
  //     reached the Worker under the exact route. Its other requests are in the
  //     observability log; that one is absent, so Cloudflare answered it without
  //     invoking the Worker. No hand-made variant reproduced it — GET and HEAD,
  //     with and without the trailing slash, bare `?`, several Accept spellings
  //     and user agents all matched the exact route correctly.
  //
  // The mechanism is still unexplained, which is the honest reason for widening
  // rather than a claimed root cause. The reference implementation routes `/*`
  // and passes that scanner; this makes the two structurally identical.
  //
  // The cost is real and bounded: one Worker invocation per data-file request,
  // returning immediately. Watch the 100k/day free-tier request budget, which is
  // now shared with dataset traffic rather than just the homepage.
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "claustodian-site",
  "main": "index.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-09",
  "routes": [
    {
      // One wildcard route replaces the previous exact `/` and `/mcp` pair.
      // index.js decides what to do per path; the pathname guard there is what
      // keeps /data untouched, not the route pattern.
      "pattern": "claustodian.dev/*",
      "zone_name": "claustodian.dev",
    },
  ],
  // No public workers.dev entry point; the route is the only way in.
  "workers_dev": false,
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true,
  },
}
